# /etc/synnet/trusted-ifaces — container and VM bridges this machine is the
# gateway for. One interface name per line. '#' starts a comment.
#
# WHAT THIS IS FOR
#
# synnet runs a default-drop input firewall that trusts anything from a private
# address (10/8, 172.16/12, 192.168/16). A container's traffic is covered by
# that rule the moment it HAS an address — but the packet that asks for one is
# not: a DHCPDISCOVER is sent from 0.0.0.0 to 255.255.255.255:67, matches no
# accept in the chain, and the drop policy eats it. The guest then has no
# network at all, and nothing in any log mentions the firewall.
#
# Naming the bridge here adds DHCP-server and DNS-server accepts on that link,
# which is the whole difference. It is deliberately NOT `allow in on <iface>`:
# trusting the interface wholesale would open every port on this machine to
# whatever the guest is running, and would fix nothing extra, because an
# addressed guest is already trusted by the rule above.
#
# The rules are matched by NAME, so listing a bridge that does not exist yet is
# fine and is the normal case — these interfaces are created when the container
# starts.
#
#   sudo synnet --trust-if <iface>     add one and apply it now
#   sudo synnet --untrust-if <iface>   take one away
#   synnet --status                    show the list
#
# Editing this file by hand does NOT change the running firewall: the daemon
# only rebuilds a chain that has gone missing, and one that is merely out of
# date looks healthy to it. Run `sudo synnet --firewall` after editing.

# Waydroid's LXC bridge (192.168.240.0/24), created by waydroid-net.sh.
waydroid0

